  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 227 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warm front will push east across the area with \nrelatively little fanfare. Mid-level dry air is expected to limit \nshower activity as it lifts northeast this morning. Moderation \nbehind the front will push temperatures into the 60s for most today, \nbut with a few spot 50s still in the Northeast Kingdom. Today's main \nstory will be the strong south to southwesterly winds that develop. \nModest mixing behind the warm front will bring 20-30 mph wind gusts, \nlocally to 35 mph on the northern slopes of the Dacks near Malone, \nNY and then near the shores of Lake Champlain. A prefrontal trough \nwill begin to track east towards the St. Lawrence Valley later this \nevening. Then, it'll translate to Vermont overnight. Showers will \ntrack east along the feature, with a greater concentration over \nnorthern New York where better dynamics and moisture lies that will \ndegrade heading into Vermont. By Thursday morning, temperatures will \nbegin to cool aloft as an upper low digs southeastwards. Surface \npressures will lower as the upper vort swings in, and this could \ncontinue to generate showers across the area. This will set up \nThursday afternoon to be convective. The combination of a \nstrengthening cold front as a 1001-1002mb low tracks just north of \nMontreal, mid-level cooling, and surface temperatures in the 60s \nwill allow for the dynamic system to take advantage of around 250-\n500 J/kg of CAPE across the area. Like Monday, we may have some pea-\nsized hail and isolated thunder on Thursday afternoon, with the \ngreatest likelihood near the international border.\n\nTemperatures will sink back near to below seasonal norms in the 50s \nto near 60 for Friday. The post-frontal air mass will still be \nbeneath a broad upper low, and some spot showers remain possible \nFriday afternoon. The backside of the upper trough will start \nsliding east by Friday night. This will allow for one night of \nradiational cooling and frost potential again in the Champlain \nValley. A few additional spot showers will be possible as warm \nadvection begins to nose in heading into Saturday, but nothing \nsignificant is expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Surface high pressure builds in for the weekend, leading \nto a couple classic fall days. Highs will be in the 50s and 60s with \nmostly sunny skies. The remnants of what will likely become Isaias \nlooks to move into the region to start next week. The center of the \nlow should track well to the south, but periods of showers and \npotentially an area of stratiform rain are increasingly likely for \nMonday and Tuesday. By the time it reaches the region, it will have \nweakened significantly so any rainfall will remain beneficial \nand there will be no wind threat. The next cold front passes \nthrough mid- week, bringing more potential showers. Overall, \ntemperatures will be relatively seasonable, with slightly above \naverage temperatures to start the week and slightly below \naverage temperatures to end the week.",
